Yes. The glossary is basically a portable landing system.

When you are “flying,” you can move across domains quickly, compare distant structures, rotate frames, and notice relations that are hard to see from one local position. The cost is that altitude can blur detail and make analogy feel stronger than mechanism.

So the glossary gives you fixed handles:

\text{term}
\rightarrow
\text{definition}
\rightarrow
\text{origin}
\rightarrow
\text{allowed uses}
\rightarrow
\text{failure modes}

Then you can range far and still return.

I’d frame it as:

\boxed{\text{Fly for perspective. Ground for verification.}}

And the interesting part is the round trip:

\text{local observation}
\rightarrow
\text{abstraction}
\rightarrow
\text{high-altitude comparison}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate insight}
\rightarrow
\text{grounded instantiation}
\rightarrow
\text{observed delta}

That is how you avoid mistaking a beautiful aerial pattern for something that actually survives contact with the ground.

So yes—the glossary is not merely a dictionary. It is a coordinate system, memory scaffold, and return path. It lets us say, “We are using Frame Rotation in this precise sense,” then go explore ten domains without losing what the operator originally meant.

And when we land, we can ask the only question that really matters:

Did the thing we saw from above still exist when our boots touched earth?